WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.740
"Jim," Long John whispered, "I know the doctor told you to run for it, and you refused.

2
00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:13.200
I saved your life, and now you've saved mine. I won't forget it.

3
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:19.334
But the doctor is being secretive about the treasure, and that makes me uneasy.

4
00:00:19.335 --> 00:00:23.264
So we'll stick together and save our necks."

5
00:00:23.265 --> 00:00:27.251
"Breakfast, mates," someone called.

6
00:00:27.252 --> 00:00:30.764
We sat in the sand and had biscuits and fried bacon.

7
00:00:30.765 --> 00:00:37.176
With his parrot sitting on his shoulder, Long John talked to his men to restore their hope and confidence.

8
00:00:37.177 --> 00:00:42.246
"It's lucky you have old Long John to think for you!

9
00:00:42.247 --> 00:00:50.173
I'm glad I let the boy say good-bye to his friend. Thanks to that, I discovered they have the ship!

10
00:00:50.174 --> 00:00:57.108
I don't know where it is yet, but once we have the treasure, we'll find out."

11
00:00:57.109 --> 00:01:00.211
He talked on, his mouth full of bacon.

12
00:01:00.212 --> 00:01:04.869
"As for the hostage, we'll keep him.

13
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:13.489
Once we're off to sea with the treasure, we'll talk Mr. Hawkins into joining and give him his share of the gold."

14
00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:19.967
The men were all smiling and eating heartily, but I pushed my breakfast away, upset and worried.

15
00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:23.593
"How can I trust him?" I thought.

16
00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:30.932
"Long John will take wealth and freedom with the pirates over a narrow escape from hanging, which is all our side can offer."

17
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:34.938
Adding to my fears was the odd behavior of my friends.

18
00:01:34.939 --> 00:01:38.614
"Why did they desert the stockade?" I wondered.

19
00:01:38.615 --> 00:01:41.369
"Why did they give the map to Long John?

20
00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:46.456
And why did Dr. Livesey warn Long John to stay away from the treasure?"

21
00:01:46.457 --> 00:01:51.698
After breakfast, Long John and his men got ready to search for Flint's buried treasure.

22
00:01:51.699 --> 00:01:55.058
"Is everyone armed?" George Merry asked.

23
00:01:55.059 --> 00:01:59.940
"All but the boy," Tom Morgan said. "Let's go."

24
00:01:59.941 --> 00:02:08.290
Long John hung a gun on his chest and one on his back, secured a cutlass in his belt, and put two pistols in his pockets.

25
00:02:08.291 --> 00:02:15.334
He tied a rope about my waist to keep me close as we walked. I felt like a dancing bear.

26
00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:22.227
We all set out—even the fellow with the head wound—carrying picks, shovels, and supplies to the shore boats.

27
00:02:22.228 --> 00:02:28.564
The pirates and I got in, and as the boats moved along the shoreline, Merry and Morgan discussed the map.

28
00:02:28.565 --> 00:02:34.972
"The red cross is too large to be exact," Merry pointed out.

29
00:02:34.973 --> 00:02:39.644
Morgan said, "The note on the back isn't clear either."

30
00:02:39.645 --> 00:02:54.136
He read it aloud: "'Tall tree, Spyglass shoulder bearing North of North North East. Skeleton Island, East South East and by East. Ten feet.'"

31
00:02:54.137 --> 00:02:59.733
"So a tall tree is the main landmark," said Merry.

32
00:02:59.734 --> 00:03:06.679
Before us was a high plateau that joined the sloping southern shoulder of Spyglass Hill.

33
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:14.296
As we rowed, the plateau merged with the cliffs of Mizzen Hill, and the top of the plateau was dotted with tall pine trees.

34
00:03:14.297 --> 00:03:17.684
"It's that one!" Dick pointed to a tree.

35
00:03:17.685 --> 00:03:23.338
"No, it's that one!" said Tom Morgan, pointing to another.

36
00:03:23.339 --> 00:03:27.438
"Wait until we get there, boys!" shouted Long John.

37
00:03:27.439 --> 00:03:33.461
"Flint's tall tree can only be found by the readings of the compass.

38
00:03:33.462 --> 00:03:37.904
Take it easy—it'll be a while."

39
00:03:37.905 --> 00:03:47.813
We eventually landed at the mouth of a second river, which ran down a ridge from Spyglass Hill, and then we climbed a marshy slope toward the plateau.

40
00:03:47.814 --> 00:03:58.646
At first, heavy mud slowed us down, but gradually, the hill got stony underfoot, and the woods became less dense.

41
00:03:58.647 --> 00:04:03.505
"Smell the air!" Dick said, breathing deeply.

42
00:04:03.506 --> 00:04:09.286
"Forget that," George Merry snapped. "Look for that tree!"

43
00:04:09.287 --> 00:04:15.903
Up ahead, each man ran around excitedly in search of the tree as Long John and I followed behind.

44
00:04:15.904 --> 00:04:20.649
I was still tied, but I had to help him so he wouldn't fall down the hill.

45
00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:27.114
We'd proceeded about half a mile when, suddenly, one of the men cried out, as if in terror.

46
00:04:27.115 --> 00:04:30.575
"Aaahhh! Aaahhh!"

47
00:04:30.576 --> 00:04:33.556
Everyone ran in the direction of the screams.

48
00:04:33.557 --> 00:04:38.295
"He can't have found the treasure," Morgan said, hurrying past us.

49
00:04:38.296 --> 00:04:41.628
We soon saw the cause of the man's terror.

50
00:04:41.629 --> 00:04:52.044
At the base of a big pine tree was a human skeleton, with a few shreds of clothing still attached. A chill struck every heart.

51
00:04:52.045 --> 00:04:58.987
"He was a seaman," George Merry stated, examining the rags. "This is good sailor's cloth."

52
00:04:58.988 --> 00:05:04.628
"Aye, but why is he lying that way?" asked Long John.

53
00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:14.703
The man's bones lay perfectly straight—his feet pointing in one direction, his hands, raised above his head, pointing in the opposite.

54
00:05:14.704 --> 00:05:21.104
"Ah, I know! Here's the compass, George," Long John said.

55
00:05:21.105 --> 00:05:26.894
"There's the point of Skeleton Island, sticking out like a tooth.

56
00:05:26.895 --> 00:05:31.085
Just take a bearing along the line of the bones."

57
00:05:31.086 --> 00:05:41.537
George Merry took the bearing. "The body points straight in the direction of the island, and the compass reads East South East and by East."

58
00:05:41.538 --> 00:05:47.917
"It's a pointer!" Long John shouted. "It's one of Flint's jokes.

59
00:05:47.918 --> 00:05:54.956
He and his six men were alone here. He killed them all and laid one down to show the way.

60
00:05:54.957 --> 00:06:00.668
Flint was a coldhearted man." Long John pointed to the skeleton.

61
00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:07.750
"Look—the hair's yellow. That would be Dennis. Remember him, Tom?"

62
00:06:07.751 --> 00:06:15.584
"Aye, I knew him," Morgan said. "He stole my knife."

63
00:06:15.585 --> 00:06:20.180
"Dennis was quite a thief!" laughed Long John.

64
00:06:20.181 --> 00:06:28.292
"They were murdered and left here," Dick said uneasily, kneeling among the bones. "It's not natural."

65
00:06:28.293 --> 00:06:33.144
"Not natural and not nice," Long John agreed.

66
00:06:33.145 --> 00:06:41.666
"If Flint were alive, it'd be six of them against six of us. But they're all bones now."

67
00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:48.319
"Flint is dead," the fellow with the bandage said, "but if any ghost could walk, Flint's would.

68
00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.055
Flint died hard—deeply drunk and asking for more rum!"

69
00:06:54.056 --> 00:06:58.077
"Aye, that he did," muttered Tom Morgan.

70
00:06:58.078 --> 00:07:06.903
"Stop this talk," Long John said. "Flint's dead and doesn't walk. Let's get the treasure."

71
00:07:06.904 --> 00:07:18.478
But the pirates no longer ran through the woods; they stayed together, anxious and quiet. The dead pirate had frightened them.

